But actually, even without comment: the loop completes to `base == len`, and then `if ((!incomment) && (!invalue)) ctxt->checkIndex = base;` sets checkIndex = len. Then next call starts from checkIndex... wait `base = in->cur - in->base; if (ctxt->checkIndex > base) base = ctxt->checkIndex;` So checkIndex gets set to len. Then when new data arrives, buffer grows by 128 bytes, checkIndex=len old. New scan starts at old len. Rescans only 128 bytes. That's linear-ish. Hmm, but there's a subtlety: `len -= 2` for sequence with next!=0 (third=0, next='/'): `else if (next) len--;`. So len is len-1.

But wait — after scanning fails and checkIndex=base=len, when the next chunk arrives, htmlParseChunk pushes data... but does anything reset checkIndex? In the script branch: after htmlParseScript... no. The checkIndex persists. Hmm.

Actually the real upstream issue CVE... Let me look at what the "removal of an important optimization" is. Let me check the git history of HTMLparser.c upstream. The commit that introduced this bug: "html: Don't scan for '</' per char". Hmm.

Alternatively, maybe the bug is different: maybe in this version, the script branch looks like this instead? Let me re-read the code at line 5839:

```c
idx = htmlParseLookupSequence(ctxt, '<', '/', 0, 0, 0);
```

OK here's the thing: In upstream libxml2 v2.4.30-ish, the code was:

```c
if (!terminate) {
    int idx;
    xmlChar val;

    idx = htmlParseLookupSequence(ctxt, '<', '/', 0, 0, 0);
    if (idx < 0)
        goto done;
    val = in->cur[idx + 2];
    if (val == 0) /* bad cut of input */
        goto done;
}
```

Hmm wait, actually at some point the upstream had:

```c
		if (!terminate) {
		    int idx;
		    xmlChar val;

		    idx = htmlParseLookupSequence(ctxt, '<', '/', 0, 1, 0);
```

Hmm no.

Let me just check the actual fix for OSS-Fuzz 23765. Search my memory: OSS-Fuzz issue 23765 - "libxml2:html: Timeout in htmlParseChunk"? or "libxml2: html_fuzzer: ...". Actually the fix is commit `a1ee04d8` ... I don't remember exactly. Let me instead verify empirically: build and test.

